Pulmonary hypertension
Cardiovascular
Exertional dyspnoea/syncope, Loud P2, Right ventricular heave, Elevated pulmonary arterial pressure

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-035
Reason for Visit: Progressive, severe shortness of breath and an episode of near-fainting while walking short distances.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute worsening of exertional dyspnea culminating in an episode of exertional presyncope. In a patient with right-sided heart strain, this suggests an acute increase in pulmonary vascular resistance and impending right ventricular failure. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to evaluate for acute hypoxemia, massive volume overload, or acute cor pulmonale, safely establishing if emergency hospitalization was needed before routine clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I can't even walk to the letterbox without gasping for air, and today I felt so dizzy I thought I was going to black out."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports progressive exertional dyspnea and reduced exercise tolerance over the last 3 months, which has sharply declined over the past week. Today, the patient experienced an episode of exertional presyncope while walking across the house. Complains of generalized severe fatigue and lethargy. Notes increasing swelling in the ankles and a feeling of fullness in the abdomen. Denies acute chest pain, orthopnea, or paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea. KEY SYMPTOMS: Progressive exertional dyspnoea and reduced exercise tolerance. Exertional presyncope. Generalized severe fatigue and lethargy. Peripheral pitting oedema in advanced right heart failure.
Functional Status: Highly restricted; fearful of any exertion due to dyspnea and dizziness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of a syncopal fall if exertion is attempted.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.5 C | RR: 22 | SpO2: 92 percent (on room air) | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Visibly fatigued, tachypneic at rest, cyanosis absent but peripherally cool.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Prominent right ventricular heave palpated at the left parasternal border. Auscultation reveals a prominently and loudly split second heart sound (loud P2). A right-sided third heart sound (S3) gallop is audible. Jugular venous pressure is markedly elevated to 7 cm above the sternal angle with a prominent 'a' wave. Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally (no crackles). Abdomen: Mildly distended with tender hepatomegaly. Extremities: 2+ bilateral peripheral pitting edema to the mid-calves. DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: Exertional dyspnoea/syncope, Loud P2, Right ventricular heave, Elevated pulmonary arterial pressure (pending formal right heart catheterization).
Environmental Observations: Clear pathways, but patient is resting heavily on furniture.
Medication Reconciliation: No specific pulmonary vasodilator therapies; currently taking an over-the-counter multivitamin.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Pulmonary Hypertension (Suspected, with signs of Right Heart Failure) - Status: Exacerbated (High Risk)
Differentials:

Left-Sided Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of orthopnea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, and bibasal pulmonary crackles; findings are exclusively right-sided (RV heave, loud P2, elevated JVP).

Acute Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The chronic, progressive nature of the symptoms over months, rather than an acute, sudden onset of pleuritic chest pain and breathlessness.

Severe Aortic Stenosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of an ejection systolic murmur at the base; the primary acoustic finding is a loud P2, pointing to the pulmonary rather than systemic circuit.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits classic, profound signs of severe pulmonary hypertension progressing to right ventricular failure: exertional presyncope, a prominent RV heave, a loud P2, elevated JVP, and peripheral edema. The onset of exertional presyncope is a major red flag, indicating that the right ventricle can no longer increase cardiac output against the high pulmonary vascular resistance during exertion, risking sudden cardiac death. Urgent physical assessment was mandatory to confirm these right-sided signs, recognize the high-risk nature of the presyncope, and mandate strict physical restrictions while arranging urgent specialized cardiovascular evaluation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advised strict, absolute physical rest. Arranged urgent outpatient transthoracic echocardiogram to estimate pulmonary artery pressures and assess right ventricular function. Ordered basic metabolic panel and BNP.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instructed the patient to actively pace all activities, stopping to rest well before they feel dizzy or severely short of breath. By respecting these physical limits, the patient avoids overloading the strained right heart. The reward is the direct prevention of a terrifying syncopal collapse.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: None.
Follow-up: Urgent General Practice review within 24-48 hours to review echocardiogram and expedite a respiratory/cardiology referral for formal right heart catheterization.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient experiences chest pain, severe breathlessness at rest, or a complete loss of consciousness (syncope), they must call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis and precise pressure measurement requires a specialized ultrasound (echocardiogram) and a right heart catheterization; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: High pressure in the lung arteries severely strains the right side of the heart. Specialized medications are often required to dilate these vessels and improve exercise capacity.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not push through breathlessness. Rest frequently. Seek emergency medical care immediately if you faint or develop chest pain.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The blood vessels in your lungs have become narrowed and stiff, creating high pressure. The right side of your heart is struggling to pump blood through them, which is causing your breathlessness, the fluid buildup in your legs, and the dizziness. You need an urgent heart scan to measure this pressure.
Actionable Steps: Rest completely over the next two days. Do not do any heavy lifting or brisk walking. Keep your legs elevated when sitting.

